nd record of outperforming the district's Republican lean, combined with ratings clustered at Lean-to-Likely D and markets pricing her near ninety percent, point to a clear Democratic edge; thin and inconsistent polling keeps the uncertainty band wide.", "watch": "Braun's closing argument, including attacks tied to his company and the workplace-accident storyline from the final debates, could tighten the race if it resonates with swing voters.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Marie Gluesenkamp Perez (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Marie Gluesenkamp Perez seeks another term in a Southwest Washington district that leans slightly Republican on partisan vote but has repeatedly favored her personally. She enters the general election with a large financial edge, raising about $6.3 million to John Braun's $2.2 million, and holds roughly twice his cash on hand. Outside spending has been minimal on both sides.\n\nMost professional ratings place the race between Lean D and Likely D, with only Inside Elections calling it a Tossup, and prediction markets price her win chances near ninety percent. Available polling is sparse and dated, much of it from before the top-two primary, and shows mixed results. The race remains competitive on paper, but the incumbent's money, incumbency, and cross-party appeal give her a clear, though not commanding, advantage heading into Election Day.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.8833, "margin": 7.32}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8392, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8323, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.8357, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.8044, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.8079, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.8641, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.8353, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.8253, "marg